      <h1>Did the devil's fingers <em>really move?</em></h1>
      <p class="discovery-deck">A strange fungus. An unexpected observation. And a question about the difference between where life exists and where we've managed to notice it.</p>
      <p class="discovery-byline">MYCOVIEW FIELD NOTES · RESEARCH STORY · BASED ON A 2024 PUBLISHED STUDY</p>
    </div>
  </header>
  <figure class="discovery-wide-photo">
    <img src={photo} alt="Distinctive red, finger-like fruiting body of Clathrus archeri" loading="eager"/>
    <figcaption><i>Clathrus archeri</i>, commonly called devil's fingers. Illustrative photograph, not the Ukrainian study specimen. Matteo Marcandella, <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clathrus_archeri_100906693.jpg">CC BY 4.0</a>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="discovery-prose">
    <p class="discovery-drop">There are mushrooms that blend into the forest floor. And then there's <i>Clathrus archeri</i>. Its bright red, finger-like arms unfold into a shape that seems borrowed from another planet. You might know it as devil's fingers.</p>
    <p>It's the kind of fungus you would think somebody would remember seeing. But remembering, recording, and scientifically documenting an occurrence are three very different things.</p>
    <h2>A dot on the map</h2>
    <p>In a 2024 study of Ukrainian fungal observations, researchers <strong>Oleh Prylutskyi and Nadiia Kapets</strong> examined how records contributed through iNaturalist could add to the country's existing fungal biodiversity knowledge.</p>
    <p>One example stood out. An observation of <i>Clathrus archeri</i> near Kyiv lay approximately <strong>350 kilometers</strong> from the nearest previously documented occurrence in the sources the authors compared.</p>
    <aside class="discovery-pull"><span>THE SURPRISING PART</span><strong>350 km between documented records is not 350 km of measured movement.</strong></aside>
    <p>It's tempting to picture a fungus marching across the landscape. But the paper didn't follow spores, track colonization, or establish when the organism first reached the area. It compared occurrence records.</p>
    <h2>What if it was already there?</h2>
    <p>A new record tells us that someone documented a fungus in a particular place. It does not tell us when the fungus arrived. Earlier populations might have gone unobserved, unreported, or unrecognized. Records may also be tucked away in local publications or collections that aren't represented in a particular dataset.</p>
    <p>That doesn't make the observation unimportant. Quite the opposite: careful citizen-science records can reveal gaps in what science has documented. The discovery may be about the fungus's distribution, about our sampling, or about both. Distinguishing those possibilities is the interesting part.</p>
    <div class="discovery-three">
      <p class="discovery-kicker">THREE QUESTIONS TO TAKE INTO THE WOODS</p>
      <ol><li><strong>What was actually observed?</strong> A fungus at a place and time—or a claim about its history?</li><li><strong>What might be missing?</strong> Earlier records, overlooked habitats, or areas with few observers?</li><li><strong>What would change our minds?</strong> Better identification, independent records, or targeted surveys?</li></ol>
    </div>
    <h2>Sometimes the discovery is what we didn't know</h2>
    <p>That's the beautiful—and occasionally frustrating—thing about biodiversity research. A photograph can add a valuable new point to the scientific record while leaving a much larger story unresolved.</p>
    <p>The authors' work illustrates why community observations matter, and why scientific restraint matters just as much. The question isn't only <em>where is this fungus?</em> It's also <em>how well have we looked?</em></p>
  </div>
